{
  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThis file input component fails at the most fundamental level of form accessibility: the input has no accessible name. Screen reader users encounter an unlabeled \"file input\" with no way to determine its purpose. Additionally, the error state is communicated exclusively through visual rendering \u2014 no `aria-invalid`, no `aria-describedby`, no live region announcement. A screen reader user who selects an oversized file receives zero programmatic feedback that something went wrong. Two of the three core form accessibility requirements (label, error association, state announcement) are entirely absent.\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, I predicted for a file input component:\n1. **Missing `<label>` association** \u2014 the visible text is likely in a non-semantic element rather than a proper `<label>` with `htmlFor`. \u2705 Confirmed.\n2. **Error not associated with input** \u2014 no `aria-describedby` linking the error message to the input. \u2705 Confirmed.\n3. **No `aria-invalid`** \u2014 the input doesn't signal error state to AT. \u2705 Confirmed.\n4. **No live region for error announcement** \u2014 the error div has no `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live`. \u2705 Confirmed.\n5. **Help text about file restrictions not associated** \u2014 the `accept` attribute exists but no human-readable restriction is linked to the input. \u2705 Confirmed.\n\nAll five predictions were confirmed. No surprises.\n\n---\n\n## Critical Findings (blocks access)\n\n### 1. Input has no accessible name \u2014 no `<label>` element\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` lines 22\u201326\n\n```jsx\n<div>Upload file</div>          // \u2190 line 22: plain div, not a label\n<input\n  type=\"file\"                   // \u2190 line 24: no id, no aria-label, no aria-labelledby\n  onChange={handleFileChange}\n  accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"\n/>\n```\n\nThe text \"Upload file\" is rendered inside a `<div>`. It is not a `<label>` element, has no `htmlFor` attribute, and the input has no `id`, `aria-label`, or `aria-labelledby`. The input is completely unnamed in the accessibility tree.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **User group impacted:** Screen reader (all), Cognitive\n- **WCAG citation:** 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value\n- **Why this matters:** A screen reader user tabbing to this input hears only \"file input\" (or \"Choose File\" on some UAs). They have zero context for what file to upload, what this form is for, or how this input relates to the rest of the page. This is a complete identification failure. Per WCAG 4.1.2, all UI components must expose a name to assistive technology.\n- **Fix:**\n  ```jsx\n  <label htmlFor=\"file-upload-input\">Upload file</label>\n  <input\n    id=\"file-upload-input\"\n    type=\"file\"\n    onChange={handleFileChange}\n    accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"\n    aria-describedby={error ? \"file-upload-error\" : \"file-upload-help\"}\n    aria-invalid={error ? \"true\" : undefined}\n  />\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings (significantly degrades experience)\n\n### 2. Error message not associated with input via `aria-describedby`\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` lines 28\u201331\n\n```jsx\n{error && (\n  <div className=\"error-message\">   // \u2190 no id, no role, no aria-live\n    {error}\n  </div>\n)}\n```\n\nThe error `<div>` has no `id` attribute. The input has no `aria-describedby` attribute. There is no programmatic link between the error message and the input it describes.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **User group impacted:** Screen reader (all)\n- **WCAG citation:** 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; 3.3.1 Error Identification\n- **Why this matters:** Even if the error were announced (see finding #3), the screen reader user would hear \"File too large\" but have no programmatic association telling them *which* input produced the error. In a form with multiple inputs, the error is orphaned. Per WCAG 3.3.1, errors must be identified in text and associated with the relevant field.\n- **Fix:**\n  ```jsx\n  <input\n    id=\"file-upload-input\"\n    type=\"file\"\n    aria-describedby={error ? \"file-upload-error\" : \"file-upload-help\"}\n    aria-invalid={error ? \"true\" : undefined}\n    // ...\n  />\n  {error && (\n    <div id=\"file-upload-error\" role=\"alert\" className=\"error-message\">\n      {error}\n    </div>\n  )}\n  ```\n\n### 3. Error message not announced to assistive technology (no live region)\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` lines 28\u201331\n\n```jsx\n{error && (\n  <div className=\"error-message\">   // \u2190 no role=\"alert\", no aria-live\n    {error}\n  </div>\n)}\n```\n\nThe error message is conditionally rendered into the DOM. It has no `role=\"alert\"`, no `aria-live=\"assertive\"`, and no `aria-live=\"polite\"`. When the error appears, screen readers do not announce it. The user selects a file, the error renders visually, and the screen reader user hears nothing.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **User group impacted:** Screen reader (all)\n- **WCAG citation:** 4.1.3 Status Messages; 3.3.1 Error Identification\n- **Why this matters:** This is a silent failure. The user performs an action (selects a file), the system rejects it, and the user receives no feedback. They will retry, select another file, hit the same error, and be stuck in a loop with no explanation. Per WCAG 4.1.3, status messages must be programmatically identifiable and announced.\n- **Fix:** Add `role=\"alert\"` to the error div (which implies `aria-live=\"assertive\"` and `aria-atomic=\"true\"`), or use `aria-live=\"assertive\"` explicitly. Note: `role=\"alert\"` is preferred for errors because it is assertive by default.\n\n### 4. Input does not announce `aria-invalid` when in error state\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` lines 24\u201327\n\n```jsx\n<input\n  type=\"file\"\n  onChange={handleFileChange}\n  accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"\n  // \u2190 no aria-invalid, no aria-describedby, no id\n/>\n```\n\nWhen `error` is set in state, the input's ARIA attributes do not change. The input does not gain `aria-invalid=\"true\"`. Screen readers that announce validation state (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver) have no signal that this input is in an error condition.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **User group impacted:** Screen reader (all)\n- **WCAG citation:** 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value; 3.3.1 Error Identification\n- **Why this matters:** Without `aria-invalid`, screen reader users navigating the form have no way to distinguish a valid input from an invalid one. They cannot build a mental model of which fields need attention. Per WCAG 4.1.2, the state of a component must be programmatically determinable.\n- **Fix:**\n  ```jsx\n  <input\n    type=\"file\"\n    aria-invalid={error ? \"true\" : undefined}\n    // ...\n  />\n  ```\n\n### 5. No focus management when error appears\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` lines 8\u201315 (the `handleFileChange` function)\n\n```jsx\nconst handleFileChange = (e) => {\n  const file = e.target.files[0];\n  if (file && file.size > 5000000) {\n    setError('File too large');   // \u2190 state change, but no focus management\n  } else {\n    setSelectedFile(file);\n    setError(null);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nWhen the error is set, focus remains on the file input (which is correct in that it doesn't move away), but the error message is not focused, and there is no mechanism to ensure the user's attention is directed to the error. In a complex form, the user may not realize the error occurred.\n\n- **Confidence:** MEDIUM\n- **User group impacted:** Screen reader, Keyboard-only\n- **WCAG citation:** 4.1.3 Status Messages; 3.3.1 Error Identification\n- **Why this matters:** For keyboard-only users, the error appears visually but focus doesn't shift to acknowledge it. For screen reader users, the error is not announced at all (see finding #3). The combination means the error is effectively invisible to non-sighted users.\n- **Fix:** In addition to `role=\"alert\"` (which handles announcement), consider moving focus to the error message or keeping it on the input with `aria-invalid` + `aria-describedby` so the user can re-read the input and hear the associated error.\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings (friction but workaround exists)\n\n### 6. File type restriction not communicated as help text\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` line 26\n\n```jsx\naccept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"   // \u2190 machine-readable, not announced as help text\n```\n\nThe `accept` attribute restricts file types at the OS file picker level, but this restriction is not communicated as accessible help text associated with the input. A screen reader user who bypasses the file picker (e.g., via drag-and-drop in a future enhancement, or via a different OS that ignores `accept`) has no programmatic notice of the restriction.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **User group impacted:** Screen reader, Cognitive\n- **WCAG citation:** 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions\n- **Why this matters:** The restriction exists but is only discoverable through the file picker UI, which varies by OS. A cognitive-accessibility user benefits from explicit instructions: \"Accepted formats: PDF, DOC, DOCX. Maximum size: 5 MB.\"\n- **Fix:**\n  ```jsx\n  <label htmlFor=\"file-upload-input\">Upload file</label>\n  <span id=\"file-upload-help\" className=\"help-text\">\n    Accepted formats: PDF, DOC, DOCX. Maximum size: 5 MB.\n  </span>\n  <input\n    id=\"file-upload-input\"\n    type=\"file\"\n    aria-describedby={error ? \"file-upload-error file-upload-help\" : \"file-upload-help\"}\n    // ...\n  />\n  ```\n\n### 7. Error message lacks specificity (no maximum size stated)\n\n**Evidence:** `BuggyFileInput.jsx` line 10\n\n```jsx\nsetError('File too large');   // \u2190 doesn't say what the limit is\n```\n\nThe error message says \"File too large\" but does not state the maximum allowed size (5,000,000 bytes \u2248 5 MB). A cognitive-accessibility user must guess or hunt for the limit.\n\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n- **User group impacted:** Cognitive, Screen reader\n- **WCAG citation:** 3.3.3 Error Suggestion\n- **Why this matters:** Per WCAG 3.3.3, when an error is detected, the error should be described in a way that suggests a correction. \"File too large\" describes the problem but not the fix. \"File too large. Maximum allowed size is 5 MB.\" describes both.\n- **Fix:**\n  ```jsx\n  setError('File too large. Maximum allowed size is 5 MB.');\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## Enhancements (best practice not met)\n\n### 8. No `<form>` wrapper\n\nThe input is not wrapped in a `<form>` element. While this may be handled by a parent component, the component in isolation lacks form semantics. If this is a standalone component, wrapping in `<form>` provides landmark context for screen readers.\n\n### 9. No `aria-busy` or loading state\n\nIf file upload involves an async operation (which is implied by the component's purpose), there is no loading state communicated to AT. No `aria-busy=\"true\"` on the container, no `role=\"status\"` for \"Uploading...\" feedback.\n\n### 10. No success confirmation announced\n\nWhen a file is successfully selected, there is no announcement to the user. A `role=\"status\"` or `aria-live=\"polite\"` region confirming \"File selected: [name]\" would help screen reader users confirm their action succeeded.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (gaps, unhandled edge cases)\n\n- **No `id` on the input** \u2014 required for `<label htmlFor>` association. Without it, the label fix in finding #1 cannot be implemented.\n- **No `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live` on the error message** \u2014 the error is rendered conditionally but never announced.\n- **No `aria-invalid` on the input** \u2014 the error state is not reflected in the input's ARIA state.\n- **No `aria-describedby` on the input** \u2014 neither the error nor help text is programmatically linked to the input.\n- **No help text element** \u2014 the file type and size restrictions are not communicated as accessible text.\n- **No focus management for error state** \u2014 when the error appears, nothing directs the user's attention to it.\n- **No success feedback** \u2014 successful file selection is silent to AT.\n- **No `autocomplete` consideration** \u2014 while less critical for file inputs, the component doesn't consider browser autofill behavior.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n### Screen reader user (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n**Experience:** Tabs to the input. Hears \"file input\" (or \"Choose File\" on some UAs) with no additional context. No label, no description, no help text. Selects a file. If the file is too large, the error renders visually but the screen reader announces nothing. The user is stuck: they don't know what went wrong, what the limit is, or how to fix it. They retry, hit the same wall, and are effectively locked out of the upload functionality.\n\n**Verdict:** Complete failure. The input is unnamed, the error is unannounced, and the state is uncommunicated.\n\n### Keyboard-only user\n**Experience:** Can Tab to the input. Can activate the file dialog with Enter or Space. The file picker is OS-native and keyboard-accessible. However, when the error appears, it is only visible. If the user is not looking at the screen (e.g., using a screen magnifier with a narrow viewport), they may miss the error. No focus shift acknowledges the error.\n\n**Verdict:** Basic keyboard operability works, but error feedback is visual-only.\n\n### Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)\n**Experience:** The \"Upload file\" text is visible, so visual identification works. The error message is visible (presumably in red). However, if the user relies on screen reader + magnifier (common for low vision), they hit the same screen reader failures described above. In high contrast mode, the error color may not be distinguishable if it relies solely on color.\n\n**Verdict:** Visual identification works, but programmatic identification fails.\n\n### Cognitive accessibility user\n**Experience:** The label \"Upload file\" is clear. However, the error \"File too large\" doesn't say what the limit is. The allowed file types are not stated in text. The user must infer the rules from the file picker behavior, which varies by OS.\n\n**Verdict:** Basic comprehension works, but error specificity and instruction clarity are insufficient.\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | No label, no error announcement, no state communication \u2014 complete AT failure |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Basic operability works; error feedback is visual-only |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Visual identification works; programmatic identification fails |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Error lacks specificity; file restrictions not stated in text |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or motion in this component |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | No color-dependent state indicators in the code (error styling is in CSS, not visible here) |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**REJECT.** This component fails the most fundamental requirement of form accessibility: the input has no accessible name. This is not a partial implementation or a missing enhancement \u2014 it is a complete absence of the label that every form input requires. A screen reader user cannot identify, understand, or successfully use this input.\n\nThe error communication is equally broken: the error is rendered visually but never announced, never associated with the input, and never reflected in the input's ARIA state. This creates a silent failure mode where the user's action is rejected with no feedback.\n\n**What would need to change for an upgrade to ACCEPT:**\n1. Add `<label htmlFor=\"file-upload-input\">` with a matching `id` on the input (fixes finding #1)\n2. Add `aria-describedby` on the input pointing to the error message `id` (fixes finding #2)\n3. Add `role=\"alert\"` to the error message div (fixes finding #3)\n4. Add `aria-invalid=\"true\"` on the input when error is present (fixes finding #4)\n5. Add help text with `id` and include it in `aria-describedby` (fixes finding #6)\n6. Make the error message specific: include the maximum size (fixes finding #7)\n\nAll six fixes are straightforward and can be implemented in a single pass. The component's logic is sound; the accessibility layer is simply absent.\n\n**Recalibration notes (Phase 8):**\n- Finding #1 (missing label) remains CRITICAL. Realistic worst case: screen reader user cannot identify the input at all. No workaround. Affects 100% of screen reader users. Detection: immediate (first tab to the input).\n- Findings #2\u2013#4 remain MAJOR. Realistic worst case: user selects a file, gets no feedback, retries indefinitely. Workaround: user might guess the error from visual inspection if they have some sight. Affects all screen reader users. Detection: user reports \"I can't upload files.\"\n- Finding #5 (focus management) was rated MEDIUM confidence. The `role=\"alert\"` fix in #3 largely mitigates this for screen reader users. For keyboard users, the visual error is sufficient if they are looking at the screen. Downgraded from MAJOR to MAJOR (kept) because the combination of no announcement + no focus shift is a compound failure, but the `role=\"alert\"` fix addresses the primary gap.\n- Findings #6\u2013#7 remain MINOR. The `accept` attribute provides a partial workaround (file picker filters). The error message is understandable even without the specific size.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. **Is this component used within a larger form?** If the parent provides a `<fieldset>` with a `<legend>`, the missing label is partially mitigated (though still a WCAG violation for the individual input). This would not change the CRITICAL rating but would affect the user impact narrative.\n2. **Is there a global error summary pattern elsewhere in the application?** If the application has a form-level error summary that announces all errors, the missing per-field `aria-describedby` is less severe (though still a gap).\n3. **Is the file upload synchronous or asynchronous?** If async, the missing loading state (finding #9) becomes more significant. If the upload takes >3 seconds, the user has no feedback that the operation is in progress.\n4. **Is this component rendered in a modal or dialog?** If so, the focus management context changes \u2014 the modal's focus trap may already handle some of the focus concerns in finding #5.",
